serversTransport: insecureSkipVerify: applies to every https:// backend of the rule in rproxy
tcp router submission: combined rule: only its HostSNI part is used
tcp router submission-alt: backend address 'not-an-address' not understood; left out
tcp router submission-alt: service postfix-pool: healthCheck is not converted (rproxy checks L4 targets with a TCP connection: health_check)
tcp router weird: HostSNIRegexp `^(smtp|mail)\.example\.org$` is not a plain suffix; not converted (rproxy takes names, *.suffix for one label and **.suffix for any depth)
tcp router weird: ALPN is not converted (rproxy L4 routes by SNI only)
tls.options.modern: maxVersion / curvePreferences / sniStrict are not converted
tcp router imap2: entry point tls13: routers use different TLS options; rproxy has one set per rule
udp router game-copy: entry point games already has a UDP router; left out
udp router nowhere: service missing is not defined
udp router nowhere: service missing has no servers; left out
certificatesResolvers.le: storage /var/lib/traefik/acme.json (acme.json) is not read: rproxy creates a new account and obtains the certificates again
certificatesResolvers.le: dnsChallenge rfc2136: set server and tsig_key_name, and write the TSIG secret into /etc/rproxy/acme/le-tsig.key
certificatesResolvers.zone: dnsChallenge acme-dns: set api_url; existing lego accounts (ACME_DNS_STORAGE_PATH) can be used as credentials_file
global.acme: check the accounts' allowed_names (the names found in the routers) and fill in every CHANGE-ME and secret file; then rproxy-api --check-config (docs/en/ACME.md)
entry point smtps: ClientIP of TCP routers applies to the whole rule (allow_from)
entry point tls13: routers use different PROXY protocol versions; rproxy has one per rule (the first is used)
entry point tls13: no certificate found; put one at /etc/rproxy/tls/tls13.pem
entry point mixed: a HostSNI(`*`) passthrough router cannot share a port with terminating routers; terminating routers left out: term
